# The public GLM-5.3-Flash table in one window (the release sitting's GLM segment): decode at depth 512
# and 1024 and prompt processing at P = 512 (and 4096 for the references), bloomery (generate_glm5next,
# plan (a) on the A6000) against llama.cpp on the two open PR branches that build glm5next (#27752,
# #27754 with NVIDIA_TF32_OVERRIDE=0 and -fa off; mainline does not build it) at --n-cpu-moe 36 and,
# for the depth-512 and P = 512 rows, at llama-bench's own fit (the fit twins), and
# exllamav3's perf.py on its EXL3 4.05 bpw quantization (another file: a table of its own). mistral.rs
# has no glm5next loader. Our context stops at 2,051 positions (the plan's dense limit), so ours has
# no P = 4096 row. Every prediction is [derived]; nothing of GLM has been timed on the A6000:
#   ours decode, host experts only   16.2..18.7 tok/s  (glmhot: 42 routed layers of a card front
#                                                       0.30-0.41 ms, a host leg of 122.7 MB at
#                                                       130-137 GB/s plus 60-80 us, and the back,
#                                                       in sequence: 53.6-61.6 ms a step; the one box
#                                                       reading, the 3090 gate run's 1,024-step feed,
#                                                       53.7 ms a step)
#   ours decode, glmcard's default   18.7..22.2 tok/s  (the id-prefix card experts, 67 of 288 on 39
#                                                       layers: a uniform 23.3 % of the 4.744 GB, 8.1-
#                                                       8.5 ms off the step, the card experts in the
#                                                       host leg's shadow)
#   ours, a router-frequency list    21.4..30.5 tok/s  (glmcard's card A/B: +32..+63 % over host-only)
#   ours pp, the step feed           at or just above the decode rate: one body a fed id and one
#                                                       readback at the end
#   ours at depth 1024 vs 512        the same within the ruler: 11 latent layers x 1,536 B a position
#                                                       more, 17 MB a step, 0.03 ms
#   llama.cpp decode (both PRs)      12..17 tok/s      (host share at 36: blocks 3-35, 4.05 GB a token
#                                                       at V4.1's llama.cpp host rate, 82 GB/s — its
#                                                       warm decode 21.0 tok/s at 3.32 GB a token
#                                                       beside 7 ms of card — 49 ms; the card trunk
#                                                       8.97 GB at 600-680 GB/s 13-15 ms; the card
#                                                       blocks' experts 1.1 GB 1.6-1.8 ms; a split
#                                                       graph's launches 2-8 ms)
#   llama.cpp pp512 (both, -nopo 1)  65..90 tok/s      (V4.1's warm 104.6 at 6 experts a token on
#                                                       33 host layers; GLM routes 8: x 6/8, the card
#                                                       part small beside the host GEMMs)
#   llama.cpp pp4096                 60..90 at ub 512; 90..250 with -ub 4096 (more rows an expert
#                                                       a ubatch; #27752's own 75 -> 272 was 5 x 3090)
#   llama.cpp #27754 MTP (server)    0.65..1.00 x its srv twin  (--spec-draft-n-max 2: a verify pass of 3
#                                                       positions reads the union of their experts,
#                                                       288 (1 - (1 - 8/288)^3) = 22.8 a layer, 2.85x
#                                                       one token's host bytes on the 33 host layers;
#                                                       at the 51-67 % acceptance #27752's thread
#                                                       reports the pass yields 1.8-2.1 tokens, so
#                                                       1.8-2.1 / (0.2 + 0.8 x 2.85); FreeToken's forks
#                                                       archived their draft on offload as a net loss.
#                                                       Its acceptance line is the row's, verbatim.
#                                                       The glmtime smoke read 23.20 tok/s @ n=16,
#                                                       depth 6, acceptance 9/10, beside llama-bench's
#                                                       17.06: above this band if the srv twin reads
#                                                       like llama-bench. The suspect term is the pass
#                                                       cost: llama.cpp's host leg runs at 82 GB/s,
#                                                       under the DRAM rate, so a 3-row pass costs
#                                                       less than its bytes)
#   llama.cpp #27754 srv (server)    12..17 tok/s      (llama-bench's decode through the server path)
#   llama.cpp fit twins, both PRs    0.98..1.00 x the hand-set twin, decode (11.8..17.0 tok/s) and
#   (lcpp2775xfit:512,                                  pp512 (64..90) alike. llama-bench's fit keeps
#    lcpp2775xppfit:512)                                the trailing blocks' experts on the host,
#                                                       predicted `overridden CPU:101 in blk 11-44
#                                                       (blk 11: 2)` (models/glm5next.sh has the
#                                                       arithmetic): the host reads 0.6..1.2 % more
#                                                       routed bytes a token than at --n-cpu-moe 36,
#                                                       0.3..0.6 ms of the ~49 ms host leg, plus -v's
#                                                       `CUDA Graph id N reused` lines, at most ~70 a
#                                                       step of a few us (<= 0.4 ms). At two rounds
#                                                       that does not separate from the twin: the
#                                                       expected outcome is the same row within the
#                                                       ruler, with the fit column as the measured
#                                                       placement. A fit row 3 % or more below its
#                                                       twin means the fit placed otherwise (read its
#                                                       column); one above it means the leading
#                                                       blocks' card experts save more than their bytes
#   exllamav3 decode (4.05 bpw)      15..21 tok/s      (its 09-15 two-card run on this box, 103 of 288
#                                                       experts a layer on the cards: 23 tok/s; here
#                                                       64 on the A6000 (-mcs 224, the most that loads):
#                                                       the held-out hit 62.5 -> 47.7 %, host bytes
#                                                       x 1.39 on 32.7 ms of a 43.5 ms step)
#   exllamav3 pp512 / pp4096         150..400 tok/s    (361 tok/s at 11k on the two-card run)
# The engines do not share a routing condition: ours feeds prose and places card experts by id or by a
# prose router-frequency list; llama-bench feeds std::rand() ids to whole layers placed by index, whose host bytes a
# token do not depend on the ids; exllamav3 feeds wikitext-2 and adapts its placement to it.
# So ours / llama.cpp: decode 0.95..1.56 host-only, 1.10..1.85 with glmcard's default; pp512 0.18..0.29
# (the step feed against a batched prefill: not a prefill to headline until GLM has one, glmpp).
kind: baseline
question: what are ours, llama.cpp's (PR #27752 and #27754) and exllamav3's GLM-5.3-Flash decode and
  prompt rows on the A6000 in one window, for the public table?
term: tok/s @ n=96, depth 512, A6000, bloomery (generate_glm5next of the sitting's tree, plan (a),
  the `512` arm of just depth-gpu-glm5next, prose ids), with glmcard landed (its default: the
  id-prefix card experts)
unit: tok/s
predict: 18.7..22.2
condition: one lease, two rounds, the GGUF arms rotated each round after a discarded warm-up, the
  exllamav3 arms after them behind a discarded load; arms 512 1024, the router-frequency-list arms at 512 and 1024, lcpp27752:512
  lcpp27752:1024 lcpp27754:512 lcpp27754:1024 lcpp27752pp:512 lcpp27754pp:512 lcpp27752pp4096:4096
  lcpp27754pp:4096 lcpp27754srv:512 lcpp27754mtp:512 lcpp27752fit:512 lcpp27754fit:512
  lcpp27752ppfit:512 lcpp27754ppfit:512 exl3:512 exl3:1024 exl3pp:512 exl3pp:4096; no row carries [cpu-busy] or
  [other-busy], and no GGUF row [cold] (the discarded warm-up keeps the file cached — both placements'
  host sets together are the 199.7 GB file, which the page cache holds; exllamav3's fault
  counts are its load's and untagged); every router-frequency-list row names card_experts > 0; every fit row carries its
  `fit` column; a llama.cpp arm that fails
  to fit is a FAIL row the runner goes past, rerun at BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='GLM_NCMOE=37 …' (the profile
  keeps a caller's value), and a fit twin whose fit failed is a FAIL row naming it; the full output to a file first. Command:
  BLOOMERY_HOLD_OWNER=<the sitting's> BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=2
  BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/glm-public.card' just depth-gpu-glm5next <the arms
  above> > <log> 2>&1. Wall 40..53 min [derived]: 35..44 for the arms before the fit twins, and 5..9
  for the four fit twins' eight runs, each 33..58 s (the glmtime smoke's lcpp27754:6 row read `wall
  18s` for a load and 16 steps; here the 512-id depth or prompt and the steps, 11..16 s, and the fit's ~10
  measuring loads, 5..25 s); without the five depth-1024 arms, 29..42
reason: sets every row of the public GLM-5.3-Flash table (llama.cpp's two PR branches beside ours,
  exllamav3 in its own table) from one window; why now: the release sitting takes every model's
  table in one window, and GLM has had no timed row
decide-in: write the rows into rig-log first, then the README's GLM section, ratios from this window
  only, each llama.cpp row naming its PR and commit and beside its fit twin (the placement column
  says which), exllamav3 as another quantization
decide-below: read the step series and the plan line: a step over 53.5 ms with card experts on the
  card means the host leg is slower than 130 GB/s or its fixed cost larger; time the leg per layer
  (BLOOMERY_STEP_STATS, the glmhostfix round) before any row goes out
decide-above: write the rows, then name the term: the host leg's rate or its fixed cost is lower
  than the derivation took, and glmhot's step model shares that term
